king for All Simples (1 point) or All Chows (2 points). Let's see if I can form chows. For example, in Characters: W2 is single. W6 and W7 could be part of a chow if there's a W5. But I don't have W5 in my hand. The previous player has a meld of W7, W8, W9. So maybe W5 is in the wall. But I don't have W5 here. So maybe not.\n\nLooking at the Dots (B): B5, B6 (two), B7, B9. B5, B6, B7 can form a chow. B9 is a terminal. So maybe that's part of a chow. But B9 is a single.\n\nBamboos (T): T2, T3 can form a chow with T4 (if I had it). T8 and T9 could be part of a chow with T7, but I don't have T7. The previous player has a meld of T5, T6, T7. So maybe T8 and T9 could be part of a chow with T7? But I don't have T7.\n\nWait, maybe I can look for a Pure Straight (16 points). A Pure Straight requires three chows in the same suit. For example, in Dots: B5, B6, B7 (a chow), but I also need two more chows. Let's see, if I have B5, B6, B7 as one chow, then maybe another chow in Dots. But I have B5, B6, B7, and B9. So maybe another chow like B1-B2-B3? No, I don't have those. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a Mixed Straight (8 points). A Mixed Straight requires chows in different suits for 1-2-3, 4-5-6, 7-8-9. Let's see: Do I have any of these?\n\n- 1-2-3 in any suit: I have T2, T3 in Bamboos. If I had T1, but I don't. The previous player has a T1 in their history? Let me check the game history. The user's history includes Play T1, and the previous player's history includes Play T1. So T1 is already played. So no T1 in my hand. So no 1-2-3 in Bamboos.\n\n- 4-5-6: Do I have any of these? In Dots: B5, B6. If I had B4, but I have B4: 3 left in unshown tiles. Not in my hand. So maybe not.\n\n- 7-8-9: In Characters, the previous player has a meld of W7, W8, W9. So maybe I have W7, but I have W7 in my hand. But I also have W7 in my hand. Wait, my hand includes W7. If I can form a chow with W7, W8, W9, but I don't have W8 or W9. The previous player has that meld, so maybe those tiles are already used. So I can't form that chow.\n\nHmm. Maybe I should look for a different pattern. Let's check if I can form a Full Flush (24 points). Full Flush requires all suited tiles in the same suit. My hand has Characters, Bamboos, Dots. So no.\n\nHow about All Fives (16 points)? All Fives requires all melds and pair to be suited tiles of number 5. My hand has B5, but others are different. Not applicable.\n\nLooking at the hand again: I have some tiles that could form chows. For example, in Dots: B5, B6, B7. That's a chow. Then in Bamboos: T2, T3. If I can get T4, but I don't have it. Alternatively, T8, T9. If I can get T7 to make a chow. But I don't have T7. The previous player has a meld of T5, T6, T7. So T7 is already used. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can form a hand with multiple chows. Let's see:\n\n- B5, B6, B7 (chow)\n- T2, T3, T4 (if I had T4)\n- T8, T9, T7 (if I had T7)\nBut I don't have T4 or T7. So maybe not.\n\nWait, maybe I can look for a Mixed Triple Chow (12 points). That requires three chows in different suits with the same numbers. For example, three 2-3-4 chows in different suits. But I don't have that.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for a Mixed Shifted Chows (4 points). Three consecutive chows in different suits, each shifted up by one. Not sure.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should focus on forming All Chows (2 points). If I can form four chows and a pair. Let's see:\n\n- B5, B6, B7 (chow)\n- T2, T3, T4 (if I had T4)\n- T8, T9, T7 (if I had T7)\n- W6, W7, W8 (if I had W8)\nBut I don't have those tiles. So maybe not.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I can look for a Seven Pairs if possible. But as before, only two pairs.\n\nHmm. Maybe I need to think about which tile to discard to maximize my chances of forming a winning hand. Since I have to discard one tile, I need to choose a tile that is less critical for my potential patterns.\n\nLooking at my hand:\n\n- W2: Single, could be part of a chow if I have W1 and W3. But I don't have those.\n- W6: Single, part of W5-6-7 chow. But I don't have W5 or W7. Wait, I have W7. If I had W5 and W6, that's a chow. But I only have W6 and W7. So not enough.\n- W7: Single, part of the same as above.\n- T2, T3: Could form a chow with T4.\n- T8 (two copies): Two copies. If I discard one T8, I have one left. Maybe better to keep both if possible.\n- T9: Single, part of T7-8-9 chow if I had T7 and T8. But I have T8, but not T7.\n- B5, B6 (two), B7: B5-6-7 chow.\n- B9: Single, part of B7-8-9 chow if I had B8.\n- J2: Single, part of a pair or a pung if I had two more.\n\nSo the B5-6-7 chow is a solid part. Maybe I can build around that. If I can form another chow in Dots. But I don't have B8. Alternatively, in Bamboos, T2-3-4 if I can get T4. But T4 is in unshown tiles (T4: 4 left). So possible.\n\nIf I discard a tile that's not part of potential chows, maybe. For example, W2 is not part of any chow. If I discard W2, maybe I can focus on forming chows in other suits.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard J2, which is a single honor tile. But maybe that's a part of a pair if I get another J2. But there are J2:4 left in unshown tiles. So possible.\n\nLet me check the unshown tiles for J2: J2 has 4 left. So if I discard J2, there are still three left. So if I need a pair of J2, I can get it. But if I discard J2, maybe another player can take it for their own hand.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W2, which is a single, not part of any chow. That might be a safe discard.\n\nLooking at the valid actions: Play B6; Play B7; Play W7; Play T8; Play B9; Play T9; Play J2; Play T3; Play W2; Play B5; Play W6; Play T2.\n\nWhich of these tiles are critical? Let's see:\n\n- B5 is part of the B5-6-7 chow. If I discard B5, I lose that chow. So better not to discard B5.\n- B6 (two copies): If I discard one B6, I still have one left. The chow would still have B5, B6, B7. So maybe it's okay.\n- B7: Part of the chow. If I discard B7, the chow is broken. Not good.\n- W7: Single. Not part of a chow unless I get W5 and W6. But I have W6 and W7. If I discard W7, I lose that possibility.\n- T8 (two copies): Discard one, still have one. Maybe okay.\n- T9: Single. Not part of a chow unless I get T7 and T8. But I have T8, but not T7.\n- J2: Single. Discarding it removes a potential pair if I get another J2.\n- T3: Part of T2-3-4 chow. If I discard T3, I lose that possibility.\n- W2: Single. Not part of any chow. Safe to discard.\n- B5: Part of chow. Not safe to discard.\n- W6: Part of W5-6-7 chow. If I discard W6, I lose that.\n- T2: Part of T2-3-4 chow. Discard T2, lose that.\n\nSo the safest tiles to discard are W2, T9, J2, or T8 (if I discard one T8). Let's evaluate:\n\nIf I discard W2, I remove a single tile that's not part of any chow. This might be the best option. Alternatively, discarding T9 or J2. But J2 is an honor tile, and if I need it for a pair, it's better to keep it. T9 is a terminal, but not part of any chow. So discarding T9 is also safe.\n\nBut which one is better? If I discard W2, I can focus on forming chows in other suits. If I discard T9, I still have T8 and T2-3 for potential chows.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard J2, I lose a potential pair. But since there are four J2 left, maybe it's okay.\n\nWait, the user's hand includes J2. If I discard J2, another player might take it for their own hand. But if I keep it, I can use it for a pair or a pung later.\n\nConsidering that, maybe it's better to discard W2 or T9. Between those, which is better?\n\nLooking at the previous players' discards: The previous player has discarded W9, W5, B2, etc. The next player has discarded B2, W5, etc. The player across has discarded B5, W7, etc. So maybe W5 is already out. So if I need a W5 for a chow, it's possible it's already discarded.\n\nAlternatively, maybe I should keep J2 for a pair. If I can form a pair of J2, that could help with some scoring patterns. For example, if I get another J2, that's a pair. But if I discard J2 now, I lose that possibility.\n\nSo between W2 and T9. Discarding W2 removes a single tile not part of any chow. Discarding T9 removes a terminal. Since T9 is a terminal, maybe it's better to discard it if I don't need it for a chow. Because terminals are less flexible. But in this case, T9 isn't part of any chow unless I have T7 and T8. Which I don't. So discarding T9 is safe.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard W2, which is a Character tile, maybe it's less likely to be needed by others. But I'm not sure.\n\nAnother angle: Look at the unshown tiles. For example, how many of each tile are left. If a tile has very few left, maybe it's better to keep it in case it's needed for a wait.\n\nFor example, W2 has 4 left. T9 has 2 left. J2 has 4 left. So discarding any of them is possible.\n\nBut maybe the key is to look at which tile's discard would give me more flexibility in forming a winning hand.\n\nIf I discard W2, I can focus on forming chows in Dots (B5-6-7) and maybe in Bamboos (T2-3-4 if I get T4). If I discard T9, I can still work on those chows.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard J2, I lose a potential pair. But since there are four left, maybe it's okay. However, if I need a pair for Seven Pairs or something, but I don't have enough pairs now.\n\nGiven that, the best options are to discard W2, T9, or J2. Between these, I think W2 is the safest because it's not part of any chow and is a middle tile. T9 is a terminal, but not part of any chow. J2 is an honor tile, but if I can get another, it's a pair.\n\nBut since I need to choose one, maybe discard W2. Because it's a single tile not part of any structure. So the answer would be Play W2.\n", "output": "Evaluation: [The hand has potential for forming chows in Dots (B5, B6, B7) and possibly in Bamboos (T2, T3, T4). Discarding W2, a single tile not part of any chow, maximizes flexibility for future draws.]\nReason: [W2 is a single tile with no immediate use for chows or pungs. Discarding it preserves critical tiles like B5-B7 (chow) and T2-T3 (potential chow). This maintains opportunities for forming multiple chows and pairs, which could lead to higher-scoring patterns like Pure Straight or Mixed Straight.]\nAnswer: Play W2"}
