g shot. \n\nAlternatively, discard T2. \n\nHmm. This is tricky. Let me try to prioritize. The most probable melds I can form are W6-W7-W8 or W4-W5-W6. For W6-W7-W8, I need W8. For W4-W5-W6, I need W5. \n\nSince W5 has only 1 left, getting it might be harder. So maybe focusing on W8 is better. \n\nIf I discard W6, I have one left. If someone takes it, I lose the chance to form W6-W7-W8. But if I keep it, maybe I can get W8. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard T6, which is part of a possible T6-T7-T8 Chi, but I don't have T7. So maybe it's safer to discard T6. \n\nWait, but the previous player has a T5-T6-T7 meld. So they have T6 in their meld. Maybe they have T8 as well? Not sure. \n\nI think the safest tile to discard is T6. It's not part of any immediate meld I can form, and it's less likely to be needed by others. So I'll go with Play T6.\n", "output": "Evaluation: The current hand has a Chi of B6-B8 and several suited tiles. To reach a winning hand, additional melds are needed. Focusing on forming a W6-W7-W8 Chi or a W4-W5-W6 Chi seems viable. Discarding T6 minimizes risk as it's not part of immediate melds and reduces the chance of others completing their hands.\nReason: T6 is part of a potential Chi but lacks supporting tiles. Discarding it preserves critical tiles for forming W6-W7-W8 or W4-W5-W6 Chis, which are more feasible given the remaining unshown tiles.\nAnswer: Play T6"}
