ly, maybe the T2 is part of a Chow with T1, T2, T3. But I don't have T1 or T3. \n\nLooking at the J2. Maybe it's part of a Pung if I get two more. \n\nBut perhaps the best path is to focus on forming more Chows in Bamboo and Characters. \n\nIf I can get a B5, that gives me a Chow with B6 and B7. Then, with the existing B6, B7, B8, that's two Chows. Then, the W6 pair. \n\nThen, the remaining tiles are W4, W7, T2, T8, T9, J2. \n\nNeed two more melds. Maybe W4 and W7 can be part of something. If I have W4, maybe a Chow with W5, W6. But need W5. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the T8 and T9 can be part of a Chow with T7, but again, no T7. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the T2 is part of a Chow with T1, T2, T3. But again, no T1 or T3. \n\nSo perhaps the best is to try to get a B5 to form another Chow. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, B5 has 3 left. So possible. \n\nSo, in my hand, I have B5. If I keep B5, maybe I can form a Chow. \n\nBut I need to discard a tile. The valid actions are to Play T9, W7, W4, B9, W6, B1, J2, B5, T8, T2. \n\nIf I discard B5, that might be a good idea because if I can get another B5, it's a Pung. But unshown B5 has 3 left. So maybe not. Alternatively, keeping B5 for a Chow. \n\nAlternatively, discarding T9. T9 is a terminal tile. If I discard T9, maybe someone else needs it for a Chow. But I don't have another T9. \n\nDiscarding W7. I have one W7. If I discard it, maybe someone needs it for a Chow. \n\nDiscarding W4. W4 is a middle tile. Maybe part of a Chow. \n\nDiscarding B1. B1 is a terminal. \n\nDiscarding J2. J2 is a Dragon. \n\nHmm. Let's think about the current hand structure. \n\nI have a meld of B6, B7, B8. \n\nIf I can form another meld in Bamboo with B5, B6, B7. Then, that's two Chows. \n\nThen, the W6 pair. \n\nThen, remaining tiles: W4, W7, T2, T8, T9, J2. \n\nNeed two more melds. \n\nPossibly, W4 and W7 could be part of a Pung if I get two more. Or maybe form a Chow with W5, W6, but need W5. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the T8 and T9 can be part 