discard it. \n\nHmm. Maybe the best option is to play T6, as it allows for a potential chi if T5 is discarded. \n\nAlternatively, maybe playing W4. W4 has 4 left, so it's less likely to be critical for others. \n\nBut W4 is a character tile. If I play it, maybe someone can use it for a chow. \n\nAlternatively, looking at my hand, if I play W4, I can keep the T6, T7, T8 to form a chow. That would be beneficial. \n\nSo playing W4 might be a good move because it allows me to keep the T6, T7, T8 for a potential chow. \n\nYes, that makes sense. If I play W4, I can keep the T6, T7, T8. If I play one of them, I lose the chance to form a chow. So playing W4 is better. \n\nSo the answer is Play W4.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Playing W4 allows retaining T6, T7, T8 for a potential chow, which is critical for forming All Chows (2 points) or contributing to other patterns. W4 has 4 remaining copies, making it less risky to discard compared to T6/T7/T8 which have fewer remaining tiles and are part of a key chow sequence.  \nReason: Prioritizing the preservation of T6-T7-T8 for a chow improves hand structure and scoring potential, while discarding W4 minimizes disruption to others' hands.  \nAnswer: Play W4"}
